WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.038 --> 00:00:03.396
Esther Perel: What worries me is that we are more and more disembodied.

2
00:00:03.416 --> 00:00:05.394
We gather less, we party less, we drink less.

3
00:00:06.002 --> 00:00:12.321
Seventy-four percent of food that is cooked in an American restaurant is eaten off the premises.

4
00:00:12.341 --> 00:00:18.418
What worries me is that we can't really deal with the messiness of human life, the way that we are called upon to do.

5
00:00:26.332 --> 00:00:38.748
[Intersections] [Esther Perel, Psychotherapist] [Sal Khan, TED Vision Steward, CEO of Khan Academy] SK: Where should we begin? (Laughter) Do you want to go first?

6
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:39.604
No, no.

7
00:00:39.827 --> 00:00:40.975
Oh, I go first?

8
00:00:40.995 --> 00:00:42.170
I will serve you.

9
00:00:42.205 --> 00:00:43.372
OK, let me see.

10
00:00:44.016 --> 00:00:50.133
I'll shuffle it up a little in case they rigged it.

11
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:55.001
If you could redesign one system from scratch, what would it be and why?

12
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.745
Actually, if you give me that card, I would put it back in the bowl.

13
00:01:00.765 --> 00:01:02.850
No, no, you don't want to answer that?

14
00:01:03.336 --> 00:01:07.618
No, because I actually don't think that I have...

15
00:01:08.528 --> 00:01:17.389
There is no system that I would redesign from scratch, but there's only one system that I know well and that is the mental health system.

16
00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:34.755
So I think that there's so much that one can do to improve the services in mental health, institutionally, training-wise, service delivery, interconnection to the legal system, to the medical system.

17
00:01:34.775 --> 00:01:37.153
It's a huge systemic redesign.

18
00:01:37.173 --> 00:01:42.658
And I actually work with very small systems: couples, families, groups.

19
00:01:43.328 --> 00:01:45.050
My systems are microsystems.

20
00:01:46.228 --> 00:01:50.050
What would you say is one system that you would redesign from scratch?

21
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.249
SK: Oh, well, probably the one — EP: It's an enormous question.

22
00:01:54.986 --> 00:01:56.175
SK: It's a big one.

23
00:01:56.195 --> 00:01:56.395
You had a good answer.

24
00:01:56.415 --> 00:02:00.048
You know, the one people would expect me to answer is the education system.

25
00:02:00.068 --> 00:02:06.360
You know, I talk a lot about that we've always known that the ideal education system is one that's very personalized.

26
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.709
I always talk about how Alexander the Great had Aristotle as his personal tutor.

27
00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:17.451
And throughout most of human history, if you had resources, if you were a young prince, you did have a very good education.

28
00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:22.674
You fast-forward to 200, 300 years ago, we have a very utopian idea: mass public education.

29
00:02:22.694 --> 00:02:30.499
But we had to make compromises — we had to batch students together, move them all together at the same pace, have some state-mandated curricula standards.

30
00:02:30.519 --> 00:02:41.725
If a student doesn't understand a concept and gets a C, too bad, that C is in their transcript forever and they move on having to build on that C as opposed to having a chance to keep learning it

31
00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:44.395
so they can get it to a B or an A.

32
00:02:44.415 --> 00:02:48.660
So I would, instead of making it one-size-fits-all, make it personalized.

33
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:59.412
Instead of making it you only get one shot to get the grade and then you build on that gap, I would make it mastery-based, which means keep learning, which you would do today if you're learning a musical instrument

34
00:02:59.432 --> 00:03:01.170
or a sport, becoming an Olympian.

35
00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.990
And I think the credential should be the same way.

36
00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.385
Higher education should be the same way.

37
00:03:06.405 --> 00:03:09.649
It should be what you know, not how long you sat in a chair.

38
00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.912
EP: But you are looking at mastery and knowledge acquisition.

39
00:03:12.932 --> 00:03:17.497
Do you include in your definition of education also the relational component?

40
00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:20.760
SK: A hundred percent.

41
00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:21.633
And I think this is something that's also been missing.

42
00:03:21.923 --> 00:03:34.038
One of the main arguments people have always given for especially why in-person schooling is so important is that's a way to build social connections, socialization, learn to deal with conflict, relationships.

43
00:03:34.128 --> 00:03:37.578
And I agree, that is where many of us develop that.

44
00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:45.271
But nowhere on your transcript does it say that you are good at collaboration, you are a good listener.

45
00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:53.596
And so I would also, and I think we have an interesting opportunity in the coming years to really -- EP: Do you know why?

46
00:03:53.616 --> 00:03:53.883
SK: Why?

47
00:03:53.903 --> 00:04:11.094
EP: Because relationship skills and relational intelligence, even in the workplace, have always been considered soft skills, and soft skills used to be seen as feminine skills, and feminine skills are often highly regarded in principle and disregarded in reality.

48
00:04:11.114 --> 00:04:13.472
And that is fundamentally changing now.

49
00:04:13.492 --> 00:04:21.774
For the first time, relationship skills and relational intelligence are actually part of the bottom line, and they're seen as hard skills.

50
00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:26.995
And if the workplace needs it, the education system is going to begin to value it too.

51
00:04:27.015 --> 00:04:36.691
SK: You know, all of the talk these days about AI and how you develop skills for AI, and we're working on some higher-education initiatives, including with TED and Khan Academy.

52
00:04:36.849 --> 00:04:48.820
And the skill, when we talk to employers, yes, they want people to know how to vibe code and do this with that model, but the one they really care about, and they're calling them durable skills now — EP: Yeah, durable.

53
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:53.801
SK: Communication, collaboration, creativity, listening skills, relationship skills.

54
00:04:53.821 --> 00:04:55.461
So 100 percent agree.

55
00:04:55.481 --> 00:05:10.555
EP: But it's interesting that it comes from the workplace and it will create, you know, in the same way that the workplace and the economy and the technological changes have constantly changed the curriculum, now it's going to change the relational aspects of education.

56
00:05:10.575 --> 00:05:14.765
SK: And one last thing, we could talk the whole time on this one question — EP: I'm going to do this.

57
00:05:15.255 --> 00:05:17.047
I'll say one last thing about that.

58
00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:22.926
because sometimes people view it as, ‘Oh, if you’re preparing people for work, it’s “vocational.”’

59
00:05:22.946 --> 00:05:25.506
And they view that as less than the pure academics.

60
00:05:25.526 --> 00:05:30.673
But this is an example of this is a skill or a set of skills that the workplace actually wants.

61
00:05:30.693 --> 00:05:36.498
And they're not calling it soft skills anymore, they're calling it durable skills, and these are human skills.

62
00:05:36.518 --> 00:05:38.222
These make you a better human being.

63
00:05:38.242 --> 00:05:42.985
So this is an example of, the vocation is asking for you to become a better human.

64
00:05:45.935 --> 00:05:51.502
What is a conversation that you think the world needs to have, but isn’t ready for yet?

65
00:05:51.522 --> 00:06:00.375
There is, I think, a high likelihood that we have major disruptions, actually, in almost every dimension of our world in the next five to 10 years,

66
00:06:00.395 --> 00:06:12.511
the most obvious place is in the world of work where anyone from an Uber driver -- you have the Waymos coming in -- to a call center worker, to a software engineer, those jobs are either going to be transformed dramatically

67
00:06:12.531 --> 00:06:14.301
or there might be shrinkage.

68
00:06:14.321 --> 00:06:18.874
You know, some people say, oh, new jobs always get created, but hope isn't a strategy.

69
00:06:18.894 --> 00:06:26.627
And if we don't do anything about it, it's not just, And this is bad enough because those people have families, they're not going to find other jobs.

70
00:06:26.647 --> 00:06:28.228
It has follow-on effects.

71
00:06:28.248 --> 00:06:41.220
But one of the biggest ones is going to go into politics, it's going to go to polarization, a lot of people think, and I agree, that a lot of polarization that we're seeing today is because of 30 years of globalization.

72
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:42.608
But that took 30 years.

73
00:06:42.628 --> 00:06:47.622
If this takes three years or even five years, we don't have the systems to do something about it.

74
00:06:47.642 --> 00:06:50.364
So I think we have to have that conversation.

75
00:06:50.384 --> 00:06:57.871
And I think there will be a reemergence of people looking for the transcendent, the spiritual, whatever you want to call it.

76
00:06:57.891 --> 00:07:06.503
Because as AI starts to encroach on the things that we used to think were uniquely human, we are all going to be looking -- One, we need the purpose in work.

77
00:07:06.523 --> 00:07:12.403
If we don't find that, hey, how am I contributing to society and how am I supporting my family?

78
00:07:12.423 --> 00:07:14.223
It's hard to think about anything else.

79
00:07:14.243 --> 00:07:17.068
But then we also want to think about what am I?

80
00:07:17.088 --> 00:07:18.756
Who am I in this grand thing?

81
00:07:18.776 --> 00:07:23.105
So those are the conversations we should be having with more urgency.

82
00:07:23.125 --> 00:07:25.525
What do you think are the conversations?

83
00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:37.385
EP: I mean, the conversation I am participating in and trying to shape as well is the conversation about relationships, because I think relationships are undergoing a massive makeover.

84
00:07:37.475 --> 00:07:48.309
The norms are literally shifting under our feet, and we don't necessarily have the skills because globalization is one piece, but social atrophy is something else.

85
00:07:48.329 --> 00:07:57.180
And so I think they are more complicated than they've ever been, certainly in the parts of the world where they are organized by negotiation.

86
00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.858
It's no longer about duty and obligation and loyalty and community.

87
00:08:00.878 --> 00:08:07.176
Then you know what you need to do, you know what you need to accept, you know what you can and cannot change.

88
00:08:07.196 --> 00:08:11.922
And you know what decisions are on your shoulders and what is decided for you.

89
00:08:11.942 --> 00:08:25.703
But if you live in a model of individualism that puts the individual in the center, and you are to negotiate and to make the decisions, then you have a lot more freedom, of course, but it demands a very different process,

90
00:08:25.723 --> 00:08:31.878
and it demands adaptability, it demands flexibility, it demands excellent communication skills.

91
00:08:31.898 --> 00:08:39.002
You know, if it's 'father decides and nobody argues,' then you don't really need to develop many skills of communication.

92
00:08:39.022 --> 00:08:43.059
But if it’s about being seen and understood and feeling valued, then...

93
00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:54.430
So I think, Interestingly, I go to a lot of places that have moonshots about everything, but there is not to be a moonshot about relationships.

94
00:08:54.575 --> 00:08:56.165
What is a family today?

95
00:08:56.185 --> 00:08:58.699
How do we define the circle of care?

96
00:08:58.789 --> 00:09:04.303
You know, what are the boundaries that people want to draw around their relationships?

97
00:09:04.323 --> 00:09:06.374
You know, you have to negotiate them.

98
00:09:06.483 --> 00:09:07.893
I found the right one.

99
00:09:07.913 --> 00:09:09.691
Am I ready for commitment?

100
00:09:09.711 --> 00:09:11.226
Do we want the same things?

101
00:09:11.246 --> 00:09:13.278
Who will wake up to feed the baby?

102
00:09:13.298 --> 00:09:14.562
Will we have a baby?

103
00:09:14.582 --> 00:09:15.097
How many babies?

104
00:09:15.204 --> 00:09:16.870
When do we want this baby?

105
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.408
Will we visit your parents?

106
00:09:18.428 --> 00:09:19.967
Will we visit my parents?

107
00:09:20.334 --> 00:09:23.539
I mean, it goes on and on, from the small to the big.

108
00:09:23.559 --> 00:09:24.748
SK: Yeah.

109
00:09:24.768 --> 00:09:27.167
EP: And now it's, what is AI companionship?

110
00:09:27.187 --> 00:09:29.587
SK: Yeah, it's wild.

111
00:09:29.607 --> 00:09:30.539
EP: Oops.

112
00:09:31.769 --> 00:09:36.033
SK: What worries you about the future of how we connect with each other?

113
00:09:36.053 --> 00:09:37.018
What excites you?

114
00:09:37.038 --> 00:09:40.222
EP: I love the fact that there is also 'what excites you.'

115
00:09:40.242 --> 00:09:47.439
What worries me is that we are living more and more in a contactless world, that you don't have to leave your house.

116
00:09:48.049 --> 00:09:48.823
You don't.

117
00:09:48.843 --> 00:09:54.212
You can make a living, You can work, you can exercise, you can feed yourself, you can shop.

118
00:09:54.564 --> 00:09:57.453
I mean, it's just an unbelievable thing.

119
00:09:57.473 --> 00:10:02.213
And that is an enormous amount of points of contact that are being deleted.

120
00:10:02.233 --> 00:10:04.722
So, you know, we are becoming more machine-like.

121
00:10:04.742 --> 00:10:07.474
It's not just that we are interacting with machines.

122
00:10:07.494 --> 00:10:11.259
Maybe the machines are going to actually resemble us quite a bit.

123
00:10:11.389 --> 00:10:14.599
What worries me is that we are more and more disembodied.

124
00:10:14.689 --> 00:10:18.025
We are less and less embodied in real life.

125
00:10:18.045 --> 00:10:20.717
We gather less, we party less, we drink less.

126
00:10:22.576 --> 00:10:28.187
Seventy-four percent of food that is cooked in an American restaurant is eaten off the premises.

127
00:10:28.207 --> 00:10:40.240
So that whole aspect of — What worries me is that we look for the same kind of predictability and perfection that the apps give us in our relationships with other humans.

128
00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.661
We want them to be just as perfect and predictable.

129
00:10:43.681 --> 00:10:50.167
What worries me is that we can't really deal with the messiness of human life the way we are called upon to do.

130
00:10:50.187 --> 00:11:04.515
What excites me is that some of our fundamental human needs are so strong that we will find ways to connect, even if it's connecting to a machine.

131
00:11:04.535 --> 00:11:15.150
You know, we will find ways to express our longings, our aspirations, our yearnings, our loss, our need for adoration.

132
00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:25.429
And what I really am interested in is the proliferation of small groups, new small groups that come together around a shared purpose.

133
00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:27.663
a shared vision, an activity.

134
00:11:27.683 --> 00:11:33.794
It's like you see that there's a fatigue of just being constantly on a screen.

135
00:11:33.814 --> 00:11:41.343
There's something about meeting another human or humans in person and doing this.

136
00:11:41.363 --> 00:11:42.761
SK: Yeah.

137
00:11:42.781 --> 00:11:44.138
EP: What do you think?

138
00:11:44.158 --> 00:11:47.850
SK: It's easy sometimes to think that you live in a special moment in history.

139
00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.936
But I do think we're living in a very special moment in history.

140
00:11:50.956 --> 00:11:59.679
A lot of people have been theorizing about entering some type of singularity for the last several decades, and as far as we can tell, we are in that singularity.

141
00:12:00.386 --> 00:12:07.909
Whether it's artificial intelligence, biotech, manipulating the human genome, I mean, all of these things are converging at the same time.

142
00:12:07.929 --> 00:12:12.778
And one of the things that defines a singularity is you don't know what's on the other side.

143
00:12:12.798 --> 00:12:14.473
So that, by definition, is scary.

144
00:12:14.493 --> 00:12:18.068
We're afraid of things that we don't understand, that we can't predict.

145
00:12:18.088 --> 00:12:22.081
And it's happening at an accelerating — And so I do worry.

146
00:12:22.101 --> 00:12:24.219
Everything you just said is 100 percent true.

147
00:12:24.239 --> 00:12:25.679
EP: But may I say something?

148
00:12:25.699 --> 00:12:26.950
I hear it a lot.

149
00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.520
This thing about we have uncertainty.

150
00:12:28.628 --> 00:12:33.888
We have always had uncertainty, massive uncertainty.

151
00:12:33.908 --> 00:12:35.521
But we had religion.

152
00:12:35.541 --> 00:12:40.769
Religion was the intermediary between — And we trusted religion.

153
00:12:40.968 --> 00:12:44.548
Trust is the intermediary between the unknown and the known.

154
00:12:44.568 --> 00:12:49.148
And religion helped you deal with the unintelligible, with why people suffer.

155
00:12:49.168 --> 00:12:55.762
Not that they won't suffer, but how to bear the suffering, and with why bad things happen to good people.

156
00:12:55.782 --> 00:12:57.819
The question of morality and evil.

157
00:12:57.909 --> 00:13:04.959
So we had a system in place that helped us with the ethical and the existential dilemmas of our lives.

158
00:13:04.979 --> 00:13:16.253
I don't think we were less or more uncertain, except for a very short period in time after World War II, when my generation had a kind of boundless optimism.

159
00:13:16.273 --> 00:13:25.870
But for the rest, I think that what we have lost are the mechanisms that we used to use to deal with our uncertainty, not the fact that we have more uncertainty.

160
00:13:25.924 --> 00:13:32.704
SK: That's a good point, too, because you're right, for most of human history, the uncertainty -- EP: Did you grow up in religion?

161
00:13:32.724 --> 00:13:33.486
SK: I did not grow up in a religious family.

162
00:13:34.035 --> 00:13:35.315
But I agree with you.

163
00:13:35.335 --> 00:13:41.551
I actually think you're going to see a revival of religion or religion-adjacent types of structures, I think.

164
00:13:41.571 --> 00:13:44.090
And I actually find that that can be exciting.

165
00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:47.088
I think that could be a good thing.

166
00:13:47.108 --> 00:13:59.320
And you're right, even though I might say, singularity, what's it going to be like in two or three years, uncertainty, the reality is, for most of human history, the uncertainty was, Where am I going to get my next meal?

167
00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:02.116
Oh, that neighboring tribe wants to come and kill us.

168
00:14:02.429 --> 00:14:06.477
You know, my child's probably not going to live until they're five years old.

169
00:14:06.497 --> 00:14:09.040
Now most of those things aren't our concerns.

170
00:14:09.862 --> 00:14:14.117
Especially in most of the developed world, we're not afraid of feeding ourselves.

171
00:14:14.274 --> 00:14:20.644
And they are these more meta-level things of human purpose, and what's that AI going to be able to do?

172
00:14:20.664 --> 00:14:33.676
But it will, to some degree, a society in which, even if you have uncertainty around your crop or where you're going to go find the next food to hunt and gather or whatever, at least you have certainty about your place in it.

173
00:14:33.696 --> 00:14:40.219
You have certainty of like — and religion can provide some of that framework, cultural traditions.

174
00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:42.529
EP: Yes, yes, rituals.

175
00:14:42.549 --> 00:14:42.850
SK: Rituals.

176
00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:49.825
And I think that's the scary thing, is that we don't know what our place is going to be in this world.

177
00:14:49.845 --> 00:15:05.669
And what excites me though, is it's an opportunity to build, rebuild, maybe resurrect some of these things that have been throughout our history and we realize we need [them]: religion, rituals, other forms of connection, culture, relationships.

178
00:15:05.689 --> 00:15:17.141
We're going to have even more abundance in terms of, serious people are talking about diseases being cured and understanding the cosmos and breakthroughs in science.

179
00:15:17.161 --> 00:15:18.507
Incredibly exciting.

180
00:15:18.527 --> 00:15:20.439
But they are all human plus AI, right?

181
00:15:20.529 --> 00:15:26.839
I think that, at this point, what needs to shift is the human versus AI has to become the human-plus.

182
00:15:27.298 --> 00:15:30.524
There is something in the power of these two.

183
00:15:30.544 --> 00:15:33.935
There's something about a thing that augments me.

184
00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:50.258
You know, if I can call you, I mean, I remember the fax and the first mobile, and I was all the time excited about the rapidity, the speed with which I was going to be able to communicate with people who were far away.

185
00:15:50.278 --> 00:15:52.094
Producer: We've got time for one more.

186
00:15:52.114 --> 00:15:53.470
EP: Alright.

187
00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:54.332
You know what?

188
00:15:54.352 --> 00:15:58.274
Read, because we want one that takes us a little bit different.

189
00:15:58.294 --> 00:16:01.015
What do we still have to learn as humans?

190
00:16:01.035 --> 00:16:03.943
A ton. (Laughter) Man, we are just ...

191
00:16:04.061 --> 00:16:09.272
Are we actually getting better at living or just better at optimizing?

192
00:16:09.292 --> 00:16:11.947
I think that is probably the best question.

193
00:16:11.967 --> 00:16:25.414
Because when I look at love and AI, I think that we, at this moment, are optimizing intimacy into something that is manageable, measurable and disposable.

194
00:16:25.504 --> 00:16:32.078
And we have borrowed metrics and markets to love, which is one of the most incredible things.

195
00:16:32.301 --> 00:16:37.494
Because if there is something that has never been defined in data, it's love.

196
00:16:37.784 --> 00:16:45.248
You know, it's a feeling, it's a condition, it's a state, it's an action, it's a verb that you conjugate.

197
00:16:45.504 --> 00:16:46.874
It's so many things.

198
00:16:47.144 --> 00:16:51.574
And are we getting better at living or better at optimizing?

199
00:16:51.594 --> 00:17:11.594
I think that from a relationship standpoint, there is something really powerful and questioning or questionable for me in how much we're bringing a technological approach to relational dilemmas, when relational dilemmas are often not problems that you solve, but paradoxes that you manage.

200
00:17:11.818 --> 00:17:13.050
SK: A hundred percent.

201
00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:18.909
You know, I think we're hopefully transitioning from an optimization state to a thriving state.

202
00:17:19.804 --> 00:17:21.374
My gut says -- EP: How do you know that?

203
00:17:22.162 --> 00:17:29.810
SK: Well, for decades now, and, you know, as a child of the '80s, Gordon Gekko on “Wall Street” famously said, “Greed is good.”

204
00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:31.821
Capitalism won the Cold War.

205
00:17:31.841 --> 00:17:36.130
You know, we all fixate on GDP, where the stock market is going.

206
00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:41.003
And, you know, I used to be a hedge fund analyst before I did Khan Academy.

207
00:17:41.023 --> 00:17:52.220
And you see that people are optimizing, but they're optimizing around the things that they can measure, which is oftentimes money or some other measurable thing.

208
00:17:52.240 --> 00:18:01.434
And it’s not that those are — EP: From a relationship standpoint, Optimizing is when people embrace romantic consumerism.

209
00:18:01.826 --> 00:18:02.178
SK: Yes.

210
00:18:02.198 --> 00:18:03.709
EP: When they commodify each other.

211
00:18:04.538 --> 00:18:08.313
And they go with a shopping list looking for a life partner.

212
00:18:08.333 --> 00:18:08.533
That's an optimization.

213
00:18:09.501 --> 00:18:10.857
SK: That is an optimization.

214
00:18:10.877 --> 00:18:11.077
EP: It's not money-based.

215
00:18:11.097 --> 00:18:16.696
SK: But I think it comes from this — EP: The mentality is capitalism, it's emotional capitalism.

216
00:18:16.716 --> 00:18:19.658
SK: Exactly, it's the same: 'What is my return on investment?'

217
00:18:19.678 --> 00:18:21.117
'I'm going to put this in and I need to maximize what I get out.'

218
00:18:21.642 --> 00:18:25.539
But I think it’s all a related culture.

219
00:18:25.559 --> 00:18:37.247
I think we're starting to get into a phase now, you know, many people in my circle who have done very well on some of those optimizations, at least, say, the monetary optimization, and they're realizing that, yes, if you're barely getting by

220
00:18:37.267 --> 00:18:42.700
and you're stressed about it, that those first few dollars have a lot of incremental benefit to you.

221
00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:55.088
But beyond a certain point, and most people are probably further than they realize — EP: Incremental benefit is a bit of an abstract concept for something that gives people survival and ability to care for their loved ones.

222
00:18:55.324 --> 00:18:56.278
SK: Well that's huge.

223
00:18:56.298 --> 00:18:59.197
That's what I'm saying, It's a huge benefit early on.

224
00:18:59.217 --> 00:19:01.098
but at some point, it's smaller and smaller.

225
00:19:01.118 --> 00:19:03.581
But they're just used to optimizing for that.

226
00:19:03.601 --> 00:19:17.579
And to your point, they might take that same mentality and optimize for their relationships and then be frustrated that they're not thriving because their relationships are — either they never find that perfect optimization —

227
00:19:17.599 --> 00:19:21.436
or they're looking for the perfect and they resist the good because they're looking for the perfect.

228
00:19:21.512 --> 00:19:22.771
Can I do better?

229
00:19:22.791 --> 00:19:23.900
Can I do better?

230
00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.254
How much more can I get?

231
00:19:25.274 --> 00:19:33.382
And when in fact, it is the greed transposed to 'can I do better', you know, 'with my hacking,' 'can I do better with who I'm living with?'

232
00:19:33.402 --> 00:19:35.434
'Can I do better on my dating?'

233
00:19:35.684 --> 00:19:39.359
It is emotional capitalism 101.

234
00:19:39.633 --> 00:19:42.532
SK: Yeah, I never thought about it that way, but you're right.

235
00:19:42.552 --> 00:19:56.374
EP: I mean, what is fascinating is how the emotional realm, the psychological realm, has entered the business world, and we talk about safety and trust in the same conversation as we talk about performance indicators,

236
00:19:56.394 --> 00:20:00.241
and we talk about vulnerability and all of these things.

237
00:20:00.261 --> 00:20:03.582
That's a whole vocabulary that has entered the workplace.

238
00:20:03.602 --> 00:20:08.465
But the same thing has happened from the workplace and the business world into our intimate lives.

239
00:20:08.485 --> 00:20:13.128
Return on investment, hedging your bets—you know—what's the deal we're going to strike?

240
00:20:13.148 --> 00:20:13.386
SK: Yeah.

241
00:20:13.476 --> 00:20:15.326
What's going to happen when we break up?

242
00:20:15.346 --> 00:20:17.060
Because we're going to break up eventually.

243
00:20:17.989 --> 00:20:21.982
EP: It's not until we die; it's until love dies.

244
00:20:22.002 --> 00:20:22.475
SK: Exactly.

245
00:20:23.475 --> 00:20:25.221
But hopefully that's changing.

246
00:20:25.241 --> 00:20:34.945
I think we are seeing, and this goes back to I think, you're going to see spirituality and adjacent things to spirituality, people hopefully invest more in each other, and hopefully we get back to thriving.

247
00:20:35.155 --> 00:20:37.457
EP: You're optimistic.

248
00:20:37.738 --> 00:20:39.047
I'll join you.

249
00:20:39.067 --> 00:20:43.969
SK: I'm hope-timistic. (Laughs) EP: I like that.

250
00:20:43.989 --> 00:20:45.901
I'll join you on the train of hope.

251
00:20:45.921 --> 00:20:46.625
SK: Let's do it.
